 @ze7536  Who are you calling kid, pal?
If you're a regular of this channel, you'll have noticed a theme where they don't focus mining performance because not only does it not align with their content and testing workflow, but they're also very much against mining farms. No one here, including myself, is against people buying GPU's for gaming or professional use and then letting the cards in their build mine 24x7 when their not using their PC. With current coin prices, it's almost crazy not to unless your electric rate is 5 times the national average. The problem is that a huge part of the audience is pissed off that the mining farms are hurting GPU availability and driving up scalping and prices, and many don't differentiate between gamers that mine with their GPU when not gaming, and miners that only mine with their GPU, Gamers Nexus isn't going to intentionally piss off their gaming / PC enthusiast audience by showing hash rate benchmarks and mining cost efficiency analysis. I'm sure that even if a large enough portion of their audience would be interested, they'd tell you that there are other channels that provide that content and to search them out.
To add to my original point, I get that mining is profitable right now, and mining farms are going to exist while it remains profitable for them to do so, but that doesn't change my opinion that GPU minimum farms are a plague, they're terrible for the PC and gaming community, terrible for the environment in their electrical usage and carbon footprint and terrible for the environment by creating tons and tons of e-waste. The only people benefitting from GPU mining farms are the miners/farm investors themselves, GPU manufacturers/AIB partners/distributors and energy companies. If I could personally kick every GPU mining farm operator in the taint 100 times, I would. Again, not talking about you or me mining on our gaming / work rigs when not in use, only the people knowingly involved in the chain of GPU mining farms.

